# Canonical Tailwind classes

Do not write arbitrary pixel utilities (`p-[18px]`, `text-[14px]`, `size-[16px]`) when a theme token exists. The Tailwind language server's `suggestCanonicalClasses` diagnostic is the source of truth — match it on the first draft.

Root font size is 16px (`1 = 0.25rem = 4px`). Convert spacing/sizing as `px / 4`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<div className="px-[18px] py-[16px] gap-[8px] size-[16px] text-[14px]" />

// ✅ GOOD
<div className="px-4.5 py-4 gap-2 size-4 text-sm" />
```

| Pixels | Class |
| --- | --- |
| 4px | `1` |
| 8px | `2` |
| 12px | `3` (`text-xs`) |
| 14px | `3.5` (`text-sm`) |
| 16px | `4` (`text-base`) |
| 18px | `4.5` (`text-lg`) |
| 20px | `5` (`text-xl`) |
| 24px | `6` (`text-2xl`) |

Named type tokens (`text-xs` … `text-4xl` in `packages/ui/src/styles/tokens/typography.css`) beat `text-[Npx]` when they match.

Arbitrary values are fine only when nothing on the scale fits: `min()`, `calc()`, `color-mix()`, or a one-off that is not a 4px increment (e.g. `max-h-[min(90vh,920px)]`). Do not invent off-grid sizes like `text-[11.5px]` / `h-[29px]` when a nearby token would do.

Never "fix" this by switching `[18px]` to `[1.125rem]` — that is still non-canonical. Use `4.5`.
